/// \file
/// \brief `snicholls::ml` arithmetic of data-parallel training across machines: the fixed-order sum of the workers' parts, DiLoCo's pseudo-gradient, its bfloat16 compression with error feedback, and its outer step.
/// \ingroup core
///
/// **Overview.** Data-parallel training runs one model on many workers, each on its own data, and merges what they
/// learn. Two ways of merging share this header's arithmetic:
///
/// - **Every step** (TRAINING.md T5): each worker's gradients are summed across workers (an all-reduce) before the
///   optimiser steps. `ordered_sum(parts, out)` is that sum's reference.
/// - **Rarely: DiLoCo** (Douillard et al., 2023, "DiLoCo: Distributed Low-Communication Training of Language
///   Models"). Every worker starts a round from the same global weights, takes `H` local steps with its own inner
///   optimiser (AdamW in the paper) on its own data, and only then sends the change of its weights, the
///   *pseudo-gradient* `global - local` (`pseudo_gradient`). The pseudo-gradients are averaged (`ordered_mean`, or
///   `ordered_sum` and a division by the worker count) and an outer optimiser steps the global weights with the mean
///   as its gradient (`diloco_outer_step`). The paper's `H` is hundreds of steps (500 in its main runs), so the
///   network carries one exchange of the weights per `H` steps instead of one per step: for clusters, or machines
///   across sites, whose network is too slow for an all-reduce every step. Its outer optimiser is SGD with Nesterov
///   momentum 0.9 and learning rate 0.7, the defaults of `diloco_config`; with momentum 0, learning rate 1 and no
///   Nesterov the outer step replaces the global weights by the workers' mean (federated averaging).
///
/// One round, as the demo runs it:
///
/// \snippet ml/diloco_demo.cpp round
///
/// **bfloat16 pseudo-gradients.** Sending the pseudo-gradient in bfloat16 halves the bytes on the wire (GPT-2
/// small's 124M parameters: 248 MB instead of 496 MB a round). Three functions fix the arithmetic, so that the
/// transport and the training library agree to the bit:
///
/// - `compress_bf16(delta, residual, out)`: each worker rounds `delta + residual` to bfloat16 (to nearest, ties to
///   even) and keeps what the rounding dropped as its residual, added to the next round's delta (error feedback:
///   Seide et al. 2014, Karimireddy et al. 2019, 1-bit Adam, Tang et al. 2021). The residual is exact, so over rounds
///   the sum of what a worker sent plus its residual is the sum of its deltas, up to the float additions
///   `delta + residual`.
/// - `ordered_sum_bf16(parts, out)`: the reducing rank decodes each worker's bfloat16 to float (exact) and sums in
///   rank order in float, as `ordered_sum`.
/// - `ordered_mean_bf16(parts, out)`: what every worker holds after the all-reduce and the mean. ts-moveables'
///   `allreduce_sum_bf16` leaves bf16(ordered_sum_bf16) on every rank, and the training library divides by the worker
///   count in float.
///
/// \snippet ml/diloco_demo.cpp bf16
///
/// **Elastic membership.** The worker count may change between rounds, a worker leaving or rejoining. `ordered_mean`
/// and `ordered_mean_bf16` divide by `parts.size()`, the count of that round's parts, so the outer step needs nothing
/// new. The outer momentum is global state, held identically by every worker, so a rejoining worker receives it with
/// the global weights by broadcast. Its residual restarts at zero: its last round's pseudo-gradient was discarded with
/// the failed round, and a residual from an older checkpoint would re-send a remainder that later rounds already
/// carried; zero loses at most one bfloat16 rounding of one delta. Its inner optimiser state is the training
/// library's choice. Restarting it at zero is the better default: a stale first moment pushes along an old direction,
/// while zero moments, bias-corrected at a late step, begin with short steps.
///
/// **Why the order of the sum is fixed.** Floating-point addition is not associative: `(a + b) + c` and
/// `a + (b + c)` can differ in the last bit, and over thousands of steps the differences grow into different models.
/// An all-reduce that adds parts as they arrive, or in the pairing its ring or tree topology happens to give, makes a
/// run depend on timing and on the cluster's shape. `ordered_sum` defines the one order, `parts[0] + parts[1]`, then
/// `+ parts[2]`, and so on, left to right in rank order, in `T`'s own arithmetic; any transport that gathers the parts
/// and sums them in that order gives the same bits, on any number of machines, whatever arrives first. The sum is
/// sequential, not pairwise: pairwise summation is more accurate for many parts, but a data-parallel sum has few parts
/// (the workers) and the order is the contract, reproduced independently by the all-reduce of a library that pins
/// core.
///
/// **Relationships.** `ml/optim.hpp` (`adamw_step`, the inner optimiser; `squared_norm` and `clip_coefficient`, which
/// merge across shards as these sums do), `ml/muon.hpp` (the same momentum convention), `stats/random.hpp`
/// (`wyrand::stream`, one data stream per worker). The transport, the all-reduce over TCP between machines, belongs to
/// ts-moveables; the device kernels that run the same arithmetic over device memory belong to snicholls-gpu, checked
/// against these functions. Group: \ref core.
///
/// **Implementations.** One each: elementwise loops over the caller's spans. Each multiplication and addition of the
/// outer step is its own statement, so no compiler fuses them into an FMA and the bits are the same on every machine
/// (CLAUDE.md, "Machine learning").
///
/// **Guarantees.** `constexpr`, no allocation, linear in the elements (`ordered_sum`: parts times elements
/// additions). `compress_bf16`, `ordered_sum_bf16` and `ordered_mean_bf16` are `noexcept`, and are their own
/// specification in float on every machine (core's integer bfloat16 rounding, no FMA possible). `noexcept` for the standard floating-point types; a number type of the caller's own throws what its
/// arithmetic throws. `diloco_config` is trivially copyable. `out` may be `parts[0]` itself (the sum accumulates
/// into the first part) but no other part; `pseudo_gradient`'s `out` may be `global` or `local`.
///
/// **Errors.** Preconditions, asserted: at least one part; every span the same size. No errors otherwise: a NaN or an
/// infinity in a pseudo-gradient travels on as a NaN or infinity (its residual 0), so the run sees it.
///
/// **Exactness.** With one worker and `{.outer_lr = 1, .momentum = 0, .nesterov = false}` the outer step computes
/// `global - (global - local)`. That is `local` to the bit whenever `global - local` is exact, which Sterbenz's
/// lemma guarantees when `local` and `global` are within a factor of two of each other (the usual case: a round moves
/// a weight a little); otherwise it is within one rounding of `global - local` of `local` (`global = 1`,
/// `local = 1e-8` in float gives 0, since `1 - 1e-8` rounds to 1). Workers with identical pseudo-gradients `d` give a
/// mean of `d` to the bit with one, two or four workers (for four, `((d + d) + d) + d` is exactly `4d`, checked for
/// every finite float), but not with three (`(d + d) + d` rounds).
///
/// **Example.** \snippet ml/diloco_demo.cpp ordered_sum
///
/// **Performance.** `ordered_sum` (four parts) and `diloco_outer_step` against the same loops by hand
/// (`benchmarks/ml/diloco_benchmark.cpp`), 1M float elements: level, within the run-to-run noise of 10% either way;
/// about 500 million elements per second for the sum of four parts and 1.1 billion for the outer step, memory bound
/// (Xeon W-3245, Release, Apple clang 17, 2026-11-19, one thread, a loaded machine). The outer step runs once a round,
/// negligible next to the local steps. The bfloat16 pair against the same loops by hand, same machine and date, 1M
/// elements, the mean of three runs: `compress_bf16` 1.11 billion elements per second (by hand 1.04), `ordered_sum_bf16`
/// of four parts 0.90 billion (by hand 0.96): level within the noise, and the sum of bfloat16 parts about twice the
/// float sum's rate, having half the bytes to read.
///
/// **Tests and demos.** `tests/ml/diloco_test.cpp`, `demos/ml/diloco_demo.cpp`.
///
/// **Dependencies.** `<bit>`, `<cassert>`, `<cstddef>`, `<cstdint>`, `<span>`, `<type_traits>`,
/// `snicholls/core/math/constexpr_math.hpp` (for the `math_number` concept), `snicholls/core/ml/minifloat.hpp` (bfloat16).
///
/// **Known issues.** The outer momentum is kept in `T`, as the weights are; the paper keeps it in the weights'
/// precision too. `diloco_config` holds `double`s, converted to `T` once per step. With bfloat16 pseudo-gradients the
/// sum's own rounding to bfloat16 (once a round, relative 2^-9 of the mean) is not covered by the workers' error
/// feedback: that would need a second residual at the reducing rank, as 1-bit Adam keeps, and is not done. The
/// residual is the worker's own state; it is not averaged.
///
/// **Status.** Experimental, new in 0.15.0; the bfloat16 functions new in 0.16.0.

#ifndef snicholls_core_ml_diloco_hpp
#define snicholls_core_ml_diloco_hpp


#include <bit>
#include <cassert>
#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>
#include <span>
#include <type_traits>

namespace snicholls::ml
{

/// \brief The elementwise sum of the parts, left to right in the order given: the reference for any all-reduce.
///
/// `out[i] = (((parts[0][i] + parts[1][i]) + parts[2][i]) + ...)`, in `T`'s arithmetic. A transport that sums the
/// workers' parts in rank order gives these bits whatever their timing or topology (see the file's overview).
/// \tparam T The number type; give it explicitly when the arguments are containers (`ordered_sum<float>(...)`).
/// \param parts One span per worker, in rank order, each the size of `out`.
/// \param out The sum; may be `parts[0]` itself, no other part.
/// \pre `!parts.empty()` and every part has `out.size()` elements.
template <math::math_number T>
constexpr void ordered_sum(std::span<const std::span<const T>> parts, std::span<T> out) noexcept(std::is_floating_point_v<T>)
{
    assert(!parts.empty());
    for ([[maybe_unused]] const auto& part : parts)
        assert(part.size() == out.size());
    const auto first = parts.front();
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < out.size(); ++i)
        out[i] = first[i];
    for (const auto part : parts.subspan(1))   // no index into parts: GCC 15's -Warray-bounds misreads one
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < out.size(); ++i)
            out[i] = out[i] + part[i];
}

/// \brief The elementwise mean of the parts: `ordered_sum`, then each element divided by the number of parts.
///
/// Divided by `T(parts.size())`, not multiplied by its reciprocal, so a transport that reproduces it divides too.
/// It is the `mean_delta` of `diloco_outer_step`.
/// \tparam T The number type.
/// \param parts One span per worker, in rank order, each the size of `out`.
/// \param out The mean; may be `parts[0]` itself, no other part.
/// \pre `!parts.empty()` and every part has `out.size()` elements.
template <math::math_number T>
constexpr void ordered_mean(std::span<const std::span<const T>> parts, std::span<T> out) noexcept(std::is_floating_point_v<T>)
{
    ordered_sum(parts, out);
    const T count = T(parts.size());
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < out.size(); ++i)
        out[i] = out[i] / count;
}

/// \brief A worker's pseudo-gradient after its local steps: `out = global - local` (DiLoCo, Douillard et al. 2023).
///
/// The weights moved by `-out` in the round, so `out` points, as a gradient does, uphill.
/// \tparam T The number type.
/// \param global The global weights the round started from.
/// \param local The worker's weights after its local steps.
/// \param out The pseudo-gradient; may be `global` or `local`.
/// \pre The three spans have one size.
template <math::math_number T>
constexpr void pseudo_gradient(std::span<const T> global, std::span<const T> local, std::span<T> out) noexcept(std::is_floating_point_v<T>)
{
    assert(global.size() == out.size() && local.size() == out.size());
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < out.size(); ++i)
        out[i] = global[i] - local[i];
}

namespace detail::diloco_
{
// Finite by the exponent bits: constexpr, where std::isfinite is not in C++20
constexpr bool finite(float x) noexcept
{
    return (std::bit_cast<std::uint32_t>(x) & 0x7f800000u) != 0x7f800000u;
}
} // namespace detail::diloco_

/// \brief A pseudo-gradient rounded to bfloat16 for the wire, with error feedback: what the rounding drops is kept and
/// added to the next round's pseudo-gradient, so nothing is lost over rounds.
///
/// Per element: `v = delta[i] + residual[i]` (one float addition); `out[i]` is the bits of `v` rounded to the
/// nearest bfloat16, ties to even (`to_bfloat16`: a NaN stays a quiet NaN with its sign, a value beyond bfloat16's
/// largest finite becomes infinity); `residual[i] = v - to_float(out[i])`. That subtraction is exact: the bfloat16
/// is `v` rounded to 8 significant bits, within a factor of two of `v` (Sterbenz's lemma; subnormals too, both being
/// multiples of the smallest float subnormal), so `v == to_float(out[i]) + residual[i]` exactly. A non-finite `v`,
/// or one that overflows to infinity, carries no residual (0): the NaN or infinity goes on the wire, so the run sees
/// it. Error feedback after Seide et al. (2014, 1-bit SGD), Karimireddy et al. (2019, "Error Feedback Fixes SignSGD"),
/// Tang et al. (2021, 1-bit Adam); low-precision pseudo-gradients in DiLoCo and streaming DiLoCo (Douillard et al.,
/// 2023, 2025).
/// \param delta This round's pseudo-gradient (`pseudo_gradient`).
/// \param residual The worker's residual, zero before the first round; updated in place. Must not alias `delta`.
/// \param out The bfloat16 bits to send.
/// \pre The three spans have one size.
constexpr void compress_bf16(std::span<const float> delta, std::span<float> residual, std::span<std::uint16_t> out) noexcept
{
    assert(residual.size() == delta.size() && out.size() == delta.size());
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < delta.size(); ++i) {
        const float v = delta[i] + residual[i];
        const bfloat16 b = to_bfloat16(v);
        out[i] = b.bits;
        const float back = to_float(b);
        residual[i] = detail::diloco_::finite(v) && detail::diloco_::finite(back) ? v - back : 0.0f;
    }
}

/// \brief The workers' bfloat16 parts decoded to float and summed left to right in rank order, in float: the
/// reducing rank's arithmetic in a bfloat16 all-reduce.
///
/// `out[i] = ((float(parts[0][i]) + float(parts[1][i])) + ...)`, each decoding exact; `ordered_sum` over the
/// decoded parts to the bit.
/// \param parts One span of bfloat16 bits per worker, in rank order, each the size of `out`.
/// \param out The float sum.
/// \pre `!parts.empty()` and every part has `out.size()` elements.
constexpr void ordered_sum_bf16(std::span<const std::span<const std::uint16_t>> parts, std::span<float> out) noexcept
{
    assert(!parts.empty());
    for ([[maybe_unused]] const auto& part : parts)
        assert(part.size() == out.size());
    const auto first = parts.front();
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < out.size(); ++i)
        out[i] = to_float(bfloat16::from_bits(first[i]));
    for (const auto part : parts.subspan(1))   // no index into parts: GCC 15's -Warray-bounds misreads one
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < out.size(); ++i)
            out[i] = out[i] + to_float(bfloat16::from_bits(part[i]));
}

/// \brief What a bfloat16 all-reduce followed by the mean gives every worker: the float sum rounded once to
/// bfloat16, decoded, and divided by the worker count.
///
/// `out[i] = to_float(to_bfloat16(s)) / float(parts.size())`, with `s` the element of `ordered_sum_bf16`. The
/// contract: ts-moveables' `allreduce_sum_bf16` leaves bf16(ordered_sum_bf16) on every rank, and the training
/// library divides by the worker count in float. The sum is rounded (to nearest, ties to even) because the
/// all-gather sends bfloat16; the division is a division, not a multiplication by the reciprocal.
/// \param parts One span of bfloat16 bits per worker, in rank order, each the size of `out`.
/// \param out The mean.
/// \pre `!parts.empty()` and every part has `out.size()` elements.
constexpr void ordered_mean_bf16(std::span<const std::span<const std::uint16_t>> parts, std::span<float> out) noexcept
{
    ordered_sum_bf16(parts, out);
    const float count = float(parts.size());
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < out.size(); ++i)
        out[i] = to_float(to_bfloat16(out[i])) / count;
}

/// \brief The settings of DiLoCo's outer optimiser, SGD with momentum: the paper's by default.
/// \ingroup core
struct diloco_config
{
    double outer_lr = 0.7;   ///< The outer learning rate: 0.7 in the paper; 1 with no momentum is plain averaging.
    double momentum = 0.9;   ///< The outer momentum `μ`: 0.9 in the paper.
    bool nesterov = true;    ///< Nesterov momentum, as the paper uses.
};

/// \brief DiLoCo's outer step: SGD with (Nesterov) momentum on the global weights, with the mean pseudo-gradient as the gradient.
///
/// For each element, with `g` the mean pseudo-gradient (already divided by the worker count) and `μ` the momentum:
/// `momentum = μ · momentum + g`; `step = nesterov ? g + μ · momentum : momentum`; `global -= outer_lr · step`, as
/// PyTorch's `SGD(nesterov=True)` with no dampening. Each product and sum is its own statement (no FMA), in `T`.
/// \tparam T The number type.
/// \param global The global weights, updated in place.
/// \param mean_delta The mean of the workers' pseudo-gradients (`ordered_mean`).
/// \param momentum The outer momentum, zero before the first round, updated in place.
/// \param c The outer settings.
/// \pre The three spans have one size.
template <math::math_number T>
constexpr void diloco_outer_step(std::span<T> global, std::span<const T> mean_delta, std::span<T> momentum,
                                 const diloco_config& c) noexcept(std::is_floating_point_v<T>)
{
    assert(mean_delta.size() == global.size() && momentum.size() == global.size());
    const T mu = T(c.momentum);
    const T lr = T(c.outer_lr);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < global.size(); ++i) {
        const T g = mean_delta[i];
        const T decayed = mu * momentum[i];
        momentum[i] = decayed + g;
        T step = momentum[i];
        if (c.nesterov) {
            const T ahead = mu * momentum[i];
            step = g + ahead;
        }
        const T change = lr * step;
        global[i] = global[i] - change;
    }
}

} // namespace snicholls::ml

#endif // snicholls_core_ml_diloco_hpp
//
//  distill.hpp
//  snicholls-core
//
//  Copyright 2010-2026 Saxon Herschel Nicholls
//

/// \file
/// \brief `snicholls::ml` knowledge distillation: the forward and reverse KL between a teacher's and a student's distributions at a temperature, fused and stable, with the student's gradient; and the variant that keeps only the teacher's top-k logits.
/// \ingroup core
///
/// **Overview.** Distillation trains a student language model to match a teacher's next-token distribution at every
/// response position (TRAINING.md T13). With temperature `T`, `p = softmax(teacher / T)` and `q = softmax(student /
/// T)` (Hinton et al. 2015, eq. 1):
///
/// - `forward_kl{T}`: `KL(p || q) = Σ p (log p - log q)`, mean-seeking, Hinton's soft-target cross-entropy less the
///   teacher's entropy (the same gradient). `∂/∂student_i = (q_i - p_i) / T` (Hinton's eq. 2).
/// - `reverse_kl{T}`: `KL(q || p) = Σ q (log q - log p)`, mode-seeking, as on-policy distillation (MiniLLM, GKD) uses.
///   `∂/∂student_i = q_i (log q_i - log p_i - KL) / T`.
///
/// Hinton multiplies the soft loss by `T²` so its gradient keeps its size as `T` changes; here that factor is the
/// caller's `scale`. Every log-probability is `(x_i - max) · (1/T) - log Σ e^((x - max)/T)`, never a logarithm of a
/// probability, so the loss is finite whatever the logits; a zero probability contributes nothing.
///
/// - `distill_loss(params, student, teacher, dstudent, scale)`: one row, the loss returned and the gradient written
///   (`dstudent` may be `student`).
/// - `distill_rows(backend, params, students, teachers, mask, losses, dstudents, scale)`: every row of a batch on the
///   backend (TRAINING.md T9), the masked rows (the prompt) 0 in loss and gradient.
/// - `teacher_topk(logits, ids, values)`, `distill_loss_topk` and `distill_topk_rows`: the forward KL against the
///   teacher's `k` largest logits only, renormalised over them, `p̃ = softmax(values / T)`: `Σ_j p̃_j (log p̃_j - log
///   q_{id_j})`, gradient `(q - p̃) / T` with `p̃` 0 off the top-k. A teacher's logits stored this way take `k` numbers
///   and `k` indices a position instead of the vocabulary. It is an approximation (the tail's mass is dropped), exact
///   when the teacher puts no mass outside its top-k.
///
/// \snippet ml/distill_demo.cpp distill
///
/// **Relationships.** `ml/softmax.hpp` (the same shifted sums), `ml/logprobs.hpp` (the mask's layout), `ml/lm_head.hpp`
/// (the logits). Group: \ref core.
///
/// **Implementations.** One each, on the host; the backend-first functions are the seam for a device.
///
/// **Guarantees.** `constexpr`, no allocation, `noexcept` for the standard floating-point types. Two passes over each
/// row and the teacher's, three with the gradient; `teacher_topk` is O(`V · k`).
///
/// **Errors.** Preconditions, asserted: `temperature > 0`; the rows' sizes agree; `k` at most `V`; top-k indices
/// below `V`.
///
/// **Definitions.** The softened softmax, the gradient `(q - p) / T` and the `T²` factor: checked against Hinton et al.
/// 2015 (arXiv 1503.02531, eqs. 1-2, §2). The reverse KL and its gradient are derived here and checked by central
/// differences; the top-k renormalisation is the usual sparse-logit form, recalled.
///
/// **Performance.** `distill_rows` with the forward KL against the same loops by hand, after a bit-for-bit check
/// (`benchmarks/ml/distill_benchmark.cpp`), 16 rows of 50,304 float logits: 31.1 ms against 30.1 ms, level
/// (Xeon W-3245, -O2, Apple clang 17, 2026-11-19, one thread, CPU time, load average about 100: a record, not a quiet-machine figure).
///
/// **Tests and demos.** `tests/ml/distill_test.cpp`, `demos/ml/distill_demo.cpp`.
///
/// **Dependencies.** `<cassert>`, `<concepts>`, `<cstddef>`, `<cstdint>`, `<limits>`, `<span>`, `<type_traits>`,
/// `snicholls/core/math/constexpr_math.hpp`, `ml/row.hpp`.
///
/// **Known issues.** A mix of forward and reverse (the generalised JSD of GKD) is two calls and a weighted sum.
///
/// **Status.** Experimental, new in 0.16.0.

#ifndef snicholls_core_ml_distill_hpp
#define snicholls_core_ml_distill_hpp


#include <cassert>
#include <concepts>
#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>
#include <limits>
#include <span>
#include <type_traits>

namespace snicholls::ml
{

/// \brief Forward KL distillation, `KL(teacher || student)` at a temperature.
/// \ingroup core
struct forward_kl
{
    double temperature = 1.0;   ///< `T > 0`.
};

/// \brief Reverse KL distillation, `KL(student || teacher)` at a temperature.
/// \ingroup core
struct reverse_kl
{
    double temperature = 1.0;   ///< `T > 0`.
};

namespace detail::distill
{
    // max, 1/T and log Σ e^((x - max)/T): log p_i = (x_i - max)·(1/T) - log_sum
    template <typename T>
    struct tempered
    {
        T max;
        T inv_t;
        T log_sum;

        constexpr T log_p(T x) const { return (x - max) * inv_t - log_sum; }
    };

    template <typename T>
    constexpr tempered<T> tempered_of(std::span<const T> x, T inv_t)
    {
        T m = -std::numeric_limits<T>::infinity();
        for (const T v : x)
            m = v > m ? v : m;
        T s = T(0);
        for (const T v : x)
            s += math::exp((v - m) * inv_t);
        return {m, inv_t, math::log(s)};
    }
} // namespace detail::distill

/// \brief The forward KL of one row, `Σ p (log p - log q)`, and the student's gradient `scale · (q - p) / T`.
/// \param c The temperature.
/// \param student The student's logits.
/// \param teacher The teacher's logits, the same size.
/// \param dstudent Written, the same size; may be `student`.
/// \param scale The factor on the gradient (`T²` for Hinton's weighting, `1/n` for a mean).
/// \return The KL, at least 0 up to rounding.
template <row S, row Tc, mutable_row G>
    requires std::same_as<row_value_t<S>, row_value_t<Tc>> && std::same_as<row_value_t<S>, row_value_t<G>>
constexpr row_value_t<S> distill_loss(const forward_kl& c, const S& student, const Tc& teacher, G&& dstudent,
                                      std::type_identity_t<row_value_t<S>> scale = 1) noexcept(std::is_floating_point_v<row_value_t<S>>)
{
    using T = row_value_t<S>;
    const auto s = as_row(student);
    const auto t = as_row(teacher);
    const auto g = as_mutable_row(dstudent);
    assert(c.temperature > 0.0 && t.size() == s.size() && g.size() == s.size());
    const T inv = T(1) / T(c.temperature);
    const auto ps = detail::distill::tempered_of(s, inv), pt = detail::distill::tempered_of(t, inv);
    T kl = T(0);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < s.size(); ++i) {
        const T lp = pt.log_p(t[i]), lq = ps.log_p(s[i]);
        const T p = math::exp(lp), q = math::exp(lq);
        if (p != T(0))
            kl += p * (lp - lq);
        g[i] = scale * inv * (q - p);
    }
    return kl;
}

/// \brief The reverse KL of one row, `Σ q (log q - log p)`, and the student's gradient `scale · q (log q - log p - KL) / T`.
/// \param c The temperature.
/// \param student The student's logits.
/// \param teacher The teacher's logits.
/// \param dstudent Written; may be `student`.
/// \param scale The factor on the gradient.
/// \return The KL.
template <row S, row Tc, mutable_row G>
    requires std::same_as<row_value_t<S>, row_value_t<Tc>> && std::same_as<row_value_t<S>, row_value_t<G>>
constexpr row_value_t<S> distill_loss(const reverse_kl& c, const S& student, const Tc& teacher, G&& dstudent,
                                      std::type_identity_t<row_value_t<S>> scale = 1) noexcept(std::is_floating_point_v<row_value_t<S>>)
{
    using T = row_value_t<S>;
    const auto s = as_row(student);
    const auto t = as_row(teacher);
    const auto g = as_mutable_row(dstudent);
    assert(c.temperature > 0.0 && t.size() == s.size() && g.size() == s.size());
    const T inv = T(1) / T(c.temperature);
    const auto ps = detail::distill::tempered_of(s, inv), pt = detail::distill::tempered_of(t, inv);
    T kl = T(0);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < s.size(); ++i) {
        const T lq = ps.log_p(s[i]);
        const T q = math::exp(lq);
        if (q != T(0))
            kl += q * (lq - pt.log_p(t[i]));
    }
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < s.size(); ++i) {
        const T lq = ps.log_p(s[i]);
        const T q = math::exp(lq);
        g[i] = q == T(0) ? T(0) : scale * inv * q * ((lq - pt.log_p(t[i])) - kl);
    }
    return kl;
}

/// \brief Every row's distillation loss on the backend: the masked rows' losses and gradients 0.
/// \param params `forward_kl` or `reverse_kl`.
/// \param students `n` rows of `V`. \param teachers `n` rows of `V`. \param mask `n` (1 for a response position), or
/// empty for every row. \param losses Receives `n`. \param dstudents Written, `n · V`; may be `students`.
/// \param scale The factor on every row's gradient (`T² / count` for Hinton's mean).
template <math::math_number T, typename B, typename P>
constexpr void distill_rows(const B&, const P& params, std::span<const T> students, std::type_identity_t<std::span<const T>> teachers,
                            std::span<const std::uint8_t> mask, std::type_identity_t<std::span<T>> losses,
                            std::type_identity_t<std::span<T>> dstudents, std::type_identity_t<T> scale) noexcept(std::is_floating_point_v<T>)
{
    const std::size_t n = losses.size();
    assert(teachers.size() == students.size() && dstudents.size() == students.size() && (mask.empty() || mask.size() == n)
           && (n == 0 || students.size() % n == 0));
    if (n == 0)
        return;
    const std::size_t v = students.size() / n;
    for (std::size_t r = 0; r < n; ++r) {
        const auto g = dstudents.subspan(r * v, v);
        if (!mask.empty() && mask[r] == 0) {
            losses[r] = T(0);
            for (T& x : g)
                x = T(0);
            continue;
        }
        losses[r] = distill_loss(params, students.subspan(r * v, v), teachers.subspan(r * v, v), g, scale);
    }
}

/// \brief The teacher's `k` largest logits and their indices, largest first, ties to the lower index.
/// \param logits The teacher's row, `V`. \param ids Receives `k` indices. \param values Receives their `k` logits.
template <row In>
constexpr void teacher_topk(const In& logits, std::span<std::uint32_t> ids, std::span<row_value_t<In>> values) noexcept(std::is_floating_point_v<row_value_t<In>>)
{
    using T = row_value_t<In>;
    const auto x = as_row(logits);
    assert(ids.size() == values.size() && ids.size() <= x.size());
    // Each slot takes the largest (value, -index) strictly below the previous slot's, so no element is taken twice
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < ids.size(); ++j) {
        std::size_t best = x.size();
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < x.size(); ++i) {
            if (j > 0 && !(x[i] < values[j - 1] || (x[i] == values[j - 1] && i > ids[j - 1])))
                continue;
            if (best == x.size() || x[i] > x[best])
                best = i;
        }
        assert(best < x.size());
        ids[j] = static_cast<std::uint32_t>(best);
        values[j] = T(x[best]);
    }
}

/// \brief The forward KL against the teacher's top-k logits, renormalised over them, and the student's gradient
/// `scale · (q - p̃) / T`.
/// \param c The temperature. \param student The student's logits, `V`. \param ids The teacher's top-k indices, below
/// `V`, distinct. \param values Their logits. \param dstudent Written, `V`; may be `student`. \param scale The factor.
/// \return `Σ_j p̃_j (log p̃_j - log q_{id_j})`.
template <row S, mutable_row G>
    requires std::same_as<row_value_t<S>, row_value_t<G>>
constexpr row_value_t<S> distill_loss_topk(const forward_kl& c, const S& student, std::span<const std::uint32_t> ids,
                                           std::type_identity_t<std::span<const row_value_t<S>>> values, G&& dstudent,
                                           std::type_identity_t<row_value_t<S>> scale = 1) noexcept(std::is_floating_point_v<row_value_t<S>>)
{
    using T = row_value_t<S>;
    const auto s = as_row(student);
    const auto g = as_mutable_row(dstudent);
    assert(c.temperature > 0.0 && ids.size() == values.size() && !ids.empty() && g.size() == s.size());
    const T inv = T(1) / T(c.temperature);
    const auto ps = detail::distill::tempered_of(s, inv), pt = detail::distill::tempered_of(values, inv);
    T kl = T(0);
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < ids.size(); ++j) {
        assert(ids[j] < s.size());
        const T lp = pt.log_p(values[j]);
        const T p = math::exp(lp);
        if (p != T(0))
            kl += p * (lp - ps.log_p(s[ids[j]]));
    }
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < s.size(); ++i)
        g[i] = scale * inv * math::exp(ps.log_p(s[i]));
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < ids.size(); ++j)
        g[ids[j]] -= scale * inv * math::exp(pt.log_p(values[j]));
    return kl;
}

/// \brief `distill_loss_topk` on every row of a batch, on the backend; the masked rows 0.
/// \param c The temperature. \param students `n · V`. \param ids `n · k`. \param values `n · k`. \param mask `n`, or
/// empty. \param losses Receives `n`. \param dstudents Written, `n · V`. \param scale The factor.
template <math::math_number T, typename B>
constexpr void distill_topk_rows(const B&, const forward_kl& c, std::span<const T> students, std::span<const std::uint32_t> ids,
                                 std::type_identity_t<std::span<const T>> values, std::span<const std::uint8_t> mask,
                                 std::type_identity_t<std::span<T>> losses, std::type_identity_t<std::span<T>> dstudents,
                                 std::type_identity_t<T> scale) noexcept(std::is_floating_point_v<T>)
{
    const std::size_t n = losses.size();
    assert(ids.size() == values.size() && dstudents.size() == students.size() && (mask.empty() || mask.size() == n)
           && (n == 0 || (students.size() % n == 0 && ids.size() % n == 0)));
    if (n == 0)
        return;
    const std::size_t v = students.size() / n, k = ids.size() / n;
    for (std::size_t r = 0; r < n; ++r) {
        const auto g = dstudents.subspan(r * v, v);
        if (!mask.empty() && mask[r] == 0) {
            losses[r] = T(0);
            for (T& x : g)
                x = T(0);
            continue;
        }
        losses[r] = distill_loss_topk(c, students.subspan(r * v, v), ids.subspan(r * k, k), values.subspan(r * k, k), g, scale);
    }
}

} // namespace snicholls::ml

#endif // snicholls_core_ml_distill_hpp
//
//  embedding.hpp
//  snicholls-core
//
//  Copyright 2010-2026 Saxon Herschel Nicholls
//

/// \file
/// \brief `snicholls::ml` embeddings: a token's row of a table, GPT-2's token plus position, and the backward pass that adds a gradient into the token's row.
/// \ingroup core
///
/// **Overview.** The first layer of a language model: token `id` becomes row `id` of a table of `V` rows of `C`
/// channels, laid out row after row in one contiguous row (`table[id · C + c]`), as llm.c's `wte` and `wpe` and
/// nanochat's `nn.Embedding`.
///
/// - `embedding_row(table, id, channels)`: the row, as a span: no copy.
/// - `embed(table, id, out)`: copies the row into `out` (`C = out.size()`).
/// - `embed_with_position(tokens, id, positions, pos, out)`: `out = tokens[id] + positions[pos]`, GPT-2's learned
///   position embedding (llm.c's `encoder_forward`). A model with RoPE has no position table: use `embed`.
/// - `embedding_backward(dout, id, dtable)`: adds `dout` into row `id` of the gradient table (llm.c's
///   `encoder_backward`). Rows of the same token in a batch add up, so workers that share a table either own
///   disjoint tokens or keep their own gradient tables and add them.
///
/// \snippet ml/embedding_demo.cpp embedding
///
/// `embed_rows` and `embed_rows_backward` are a whole sequence's embeddings and their gradients on a backend (TRAINING.md
/// T9), 32-bit token ids, the backward adding in token order; a device library overloads them.
///
/// \snippet ml/embedding_demo.cpp embedding_rows
///
/// **Relationships.** `ml/row.hpp`; `ml/norm.hpp` and `ml/rope.hpp` come next in the model. Group: \ref core.
///
/// **Implementations.** One each.
///
/// **Guarantees.** `constexpr`, no allocation, linear in `C`. `embedding_row` refers to the caller's table and
/// lives as long as it does.
///
/// **Errors.** Preconditions, asserted: the row `id` (and `pos`) lies inside its table, and `dout` has `C`
/// channels.
///
/// **Performance.** Not benchmarked: one row copied or added.
///
/// **Tests and demos.** `tests/ml/embedding_test.cpp`, `demos/ml/embedding_demo.cpp`.
///
/// **Dependencies.** `<cassert>`, `<concepts>`, `<cstddef>`, `<span>`, `ml/row.hpp`.
///
/// **Known issues.** None.
///
/// **Status.** Experimental, new in 0.7.0.

#ifndef snicholls_core_ml_embedding_hpp
#define snicholls_core_ml_embedding_hpp


#include <cassert>
#include <concepts>
#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>
#include <span>
#include <type_traits>

namespace snicholls::ml
{

/// \brief Row `id` of a table of rows of `channels`, as a span over the table.
/// \param table The rows, end to end.
/// \param id The row.
/// \param channels The channels of a row.
/// \return A span of `channels` elements.
template <row Table>
constexpr std::span<const row_value_t<Table>> embedding_row(const Table& table, std::size_t id, std::size_t channels)
{
    const auto t = as_row(table);
    assert((id + 1) * channels <= t.size());
    return t.subspan(id * channels, channels);
}

/// \brief Copies row `id` of a table into `out`, whose size is the channels.
/// \param table The rows, end to end.
/// \param id The token.
/// \param out The embedding.
template <row Table, mutable_row Out>
    requires std::same_as<row_value_t<Table>, row_value_t<Out>>
constexpr void embed(const Table& table, std::size_t id, Out&& out)
{
    const auto y = as_mutable_row(out);
    const auto r = embedding_row(table, id, y.size());
    for (std::size_t c = 0; c < y.size(); ++c)
        y[c] = r[c];
}

/// \brief GPT-2's input: `out = tokens[id] + positions[pos]`.
/// \param tokens The token table.
/// \param id The token.
/// \param positions The position table, with the same channels.
/// \param pos The position.
/// \param out The embedding.
template <row Tokens, row Positions, mutable_row Out>
    requires std::same_as<row_value_t<Tokens>, row_value_t<Out>> && std::same_as<row_value_t<Positions>, row_value_t<Out>>
constexpr void embed_with_position(const Tokens& tokens, std::size_t id, const Positions& positions, std::size_t pos, Out&& out)
{
    const auto y = as_mutable_row(out);
    const auto t = embedding_row(tokens, id, y.size());
    const auto p = embedding_row(positions, pos, y.size());
    for (std::size_t c = 0; c < y.size(); ++c)
        y[c] = t[c] + p[c];
}

/// \brief The backward pass of an embedding: adds `dout` into row `id` of `dtable`.
/// \param dout The gradient in the embedding, `C` channels.
/// \param id The token (or the position, for a position table).
/// \param dtable The gradient of the table: accumulated into.
template <row D, mutable_row DTable>
    requires std::same_as<row_value_t<D>, row_value_t<DTable>>
constexpr void embedding_backward(const D& dout, std::size_t id, DTable&& dtable)
{
    const auto d = as_row(dout);
    const auto t = as_mutable_row(dtable);
    assert((id + 1) * d.size() <= t.size());
    for (std::size_t c = 0; c < d.size(); ++c)
        t[id * d.size() + c] += d[c];
}

/// \brief A sequence's embeddings on the backend (TRAINING.md T9): `out_t = table[ids_t] + positions_t`.
/// \param table The token table, `V` rows of `d`.
/// \param positions The position rows, one per token (`n · d`), or empty.
/// \param ids `n` token ids.
/// \param out Receives `n` rows of `d`.
template <typename B, math::math_number T>
constexpr void embed_rows(const B&, std::type_identity_t<std::span<const T>> table, std::type_identity_t<std::span<const T>> positions,
                          std::span<const std::uint32_t> ids, std::span<T> out)
{
    const std::size_t n = ids.size();
    assert(n == 0 || (out.size() % n == 0 && (positions.empty() || positions.size() == out.size())));
    const std::size_t d = n == 0 ? 0 : out.size() / n;
    for (std::size_t t = 0; t < n; ++t)
        for (std::size_t c = 0; c < d; ++c)
            out[t * d + c] = positions.empty() ? table[ids[t] * d + c] : table[ids[t] * d + c] + positions[t * d + c];
}

/// \brief The backward pass of `embed_rows`, on the backend: each row of `dout` added into its token's row of
/// `dtable`, in token order (as `embedding_backward`), and into its position's row of `dpositions`.
/// \param dout `n` rows of `d`. \param ids `n` token ids. \param dtable Added into. \param dpositions Added into, or empty.
template <typename B, math::math_number T>
constexpr void embed_rows_backward(const B&, std::type_identity_t<std::span<const T>> dout, std::span<const std::uint32_t> ids,
                                   std::span<T> dtable, std::type_identity_t<std::span<T>> dpositions)
{
    const std::size_t n = ids.size();
    assert(n == 0 || (dout.size() % n == 0 && (dpositions.empty() || dpositions.size() == dout.size())));
    const std::size_t d = n == 0 ? 0 : dout.size() / n;
    for (std::size_t t = 0; t < n; ++t)
        for (std::size_t c = 0; c < d; ++c) {
            dtable[ids[t] * d + c] += dout[t * d + c];
            if (!dpositions.empty())
                dpositions[t * d + c] += dout[t * d + c];
        }
}

} // namespace snicholls::ml

#endif // snicholls_core_ml_embedding_hpp
